    "question": {
      ":question/id": "92477238-fcf4-57c0-a788-d4cbd2b1a3bc",
      ":question/problem": "![](images/65/6504ab3dde96670102e5046887707416495895dc3b85a514b5bbe7b526b4fa42.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse $S$ and vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero\n- The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is zero\n- The flux of $F$ across $C$ is positive\n\nSelect all that apply.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\u222e}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nTo understand the flux intuitively, we imagine that $F$ represents a stream of flowing water. The flux of $F$ across $C$ represents the net flow of water escaping the region bounded by $C:$ \n\n- If more water flows out through $C$ than in through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *out* through $C$), the flux is *positive*.\n\n- If more water flows in through $C$ than out through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *in* through $C$), the flux is *negative*.\n\n![](images/a7/a72dc9866562a42a49590fd169424307675b2c14b36833bd729026f46b677eec.png)\n\n- If the net flow through $C$ is zero then the flux equals zero.\n\n![](images/3a/3a6ac8656b2e0b5cc482a16eb3a4fab57cb681c9a40cf634da47f9a4323d9fdf.png)\n\nFor an arbitrary closed curve, the outward-pointing unit normal vector tells us which direction is \"out.\"\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement III is false. Note that the flux $F$ that flows in through $C$ is the same that flows out through $C.$ Therefore, there is no net flow of $F$ through $C.$\n\n![](images/9a/9af12f28947e46810074574dad9f6b9a8b572f116456807bf9c478d12da19ec2.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero, and we must have\n\n$$\n{\u222e}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. When measuring flux through a closed curve, the orientation of the curve does not matter because the outward-pointing unit normal vector is the same at each point on $C$ in either case.\n\n![](images/c4/c4402595afc4ce12937794f0755a446ae738d3b47d7e9f4978b6344f9ef8909a.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $-C$ is zero, and we must have\n\n$$\n{\u222e}_{-C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
